  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "The lines \\[x = \\tfrac14 y + a, \\qquad y = \\tfrac14 x + b\\] intersect at the point \\((1, 2).\\) What is \\(a + b?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{9}{4}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The intersection point lies on both lines, so \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=2\\) must satisfy both equations. Substitution gives\n\\[\n1=\\frac14(2)+a,\n\\qquad\n2=\\frac14(1)+b.\n\\]\nSubtracting the known term in each equation yields\n\\[\na=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nb=\\frac74.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\na+b=\\frac12+\\frac74=\\frac94,\n\\]\nwhich is choice E.",
